  "transcript": "# Joint session with the German Foreign Affairs Committee\n\n## Why the Kensington Treaty matters to parliamentarians\n> Both committees want to hold governments to account and turn the Kensington Treaty into tangible activity, including parliamentary and civil-society links.\n\nChair: Today we’ve met after the Kensington Treaty was signed. Our aim is to work together as parliamentarians to scrutinise our governments and push the treaty from paper into action.\nArmin Laschet: Parliaments have different parties and debates; the treaty was meant to add a parliamentary dimension to government co‑operation so we can talk about Ukraine, the economy and our future relations.\nFleur Anderson: We want close ties with Europe and with Germany. The treaty covers defence, economy, climate, youth exchanges and more—parliaments should help ensure delivery.\nJürgen Hardt: We wanted a friendship treaty after Brexit. Kensington gives government co‑operation plus a parliamentary and civil society pillar. That’s what we asked for.\n\n\n## Youth, school trips and building long-term ties\n> All sides flagged youth exchanges as vital; practical fixes like a student travel list and a formal UK–Germany parliamentary exchange were proposed.\n\nFleur Anderson: I'd like school trips to be visa-free again so young people can build those lifelong memories—this matters as much as defence and trade for the future relationship.\nAwet Tesfaiesus: Visiting another country while young creates lasting ties. I worry we’ve lost some closeness since Brexit; restoring school exchanges is important.\nJürgen Hardt: Teachers struggled when every pupil needed a passport. I’ve heard of a new ‘list’ system where a class list suffices; I’ll check whether it works in practice.\nStephan Mayer: We should build a small parliamentary exchange like the US Congress programme, and start a British‑German youth work organisation similar to those with France or Poland.\nFleur Anderson: Good—those are practical ideas for turning the treaty into programmes people experience.\n\n\n## Brexit’s practical effects and the question of rejoining\n> German speakers warned leaving the EU weakens bargaining power and burdens UK businesses with EU rules; British MPs warned reopening the referendum debate risks political division.\n\nEdward Morello: The PM wants closer ties with the EU. Is full membership an option, or are there other ways the UK and EU—especially Germany—should cooperate more closely?\nArmin Laschet: It depends on politics. But the world changed after 2016: competition with China and Russia means Europe needs the single market’s power; the UK matters and we must find close ways to work with it.\nJürgen Hardt: Sovereignty can gain leverage by acting together. Germany’s trade interests are handled at EU level; being in a single market gives stronger negotiating power than any single country alone.\nFleur Anderson: There will be different views in our parliament. Repairing Brexit’s obvious problems, like SAFE, is urgent—sometimes the EU says no and that blocks practical fixes.\nStefan Keuter: My party congratulated the UK on leaving the EU. We favour reform of the EU and are interested in whether your country might want closer ties or rejoining; I’ve heard varied views.\nAphra Brandreth: We had a democratic referendum. Reopening that fight could hold us back from practical cooperation; we should focus where we can work together now, like security and energy.\nAlex Ballinger: Businesses have struggled exporting to the EU; many products now must meet EU rules, so small firms effectively follow EU standards without a vote on them—people notice the costs.\nJürgen Hardt: Exactly—if the UK follows EU rules without being at the table, people may later decide being in the decision-making process is better. Our door is open, but it's the UK's choice.\nChair: Some of us think Brexit was a mistake, and there will be a public debate. But in the meantime we need Brussels to give tangible results so voters see benefits from closer ties.\n\n\n## Security, Ukraine and differing German views\n> All agreed the UK–Germany partnership is vital for security, but German parties disagree over arms, diplomacy and social trade-offs; committee members urged both military support and a push for negotiations.\n\nDan Carden: We’ve strengthened working relations for security since Brexit. Can parliamentary dialogue in Europe focus on making post‑Brexit cooperation meet our security challenges more effectively?\nLea Reisner: My party supports Ukraine but stresses we also need to work out how to end the war—more diplomacy and mediation—not just rearmament. Climate and social security matter too.\nStefan Keuter: Our Opposition view is that the conflict began earlier and that buying cheap Russian gas would help reduce energy pain at home; we don’t see this as ‘our war’ in the same way.\nChair: Do you accept Germany was a guarantor of Minsk? If energy ties were restored with Russia, what happens to Ukraine?\nStefan Keuter: I don’t deny the war is wrong. I’m saying the roots are older, and political context matters; I argue for realistic debate on energy and costs for our people.\nAdis Ahmetović: We stand with Ukraine. But German society’s support has fallen and right‑wing or left‑populist parties exploit that. We must show socially that supporting Ukraine doesn’t mean cutting vital domestic programs.\nJürgen Hardt: Our strategy should be to strengthen Ukraine so Putin faces the prospect of failure and comes to negotiations. We also need the UK in NATO procurement and defence cooperation—UK industry matters.\nAlex Ballinger: Across UK politics there’s unity in backing Ukraine. It’s vital we keep that, while also tackling costs for households and businesses.\nLea Reisner: Security is broader than weapons; an ICU nurse, or a single parent worried about rent, has a form of security too. We must balance defence spending with social protection.\nArmin Laschet: There are real differences in German parties—some oppose weapons. But our government says: support Ukraine as needed. We also need to reassert European diplomacy so we aren’t sidelined on big talks.\nChair: Thank you all. This historic joint session showed many areas where we can collaborate—youth links, trade fixes, defence cooperation and shared diplomacy—and where national politics shape different priorities.\n",
